cgroup: expose prefixed control-file names under CGRP_ROOT_NOPREFIX

Android mounts its cgroup v1 hierarchies with CGRP_ROOT_NOPREFIX, so
cgroup_file_name() writes the bare cft->name -- "shares" rather than
"cpu.shares", "limit_in_bytes" rather than "memory.limit_in_bytes".

runc/libcontainer looks for the prefixed spellings, so on Android every
container resource limit silently does nothing: dockerd starts, containers run,
and --memory / --cpus are quietly ignored.

Add a symlink under the prefixed name beside each control file when the
hierarchy is NOPREFIX, so both spellings resolve. Android's own userspace keeps
using the bare names it already expects.

Adapted from tomxi1997/lxc-docker-support-for-android for kernels that still
have the monolithic kernel/cgroup.c rather than kernel/cgroup/cgroup.c. Uses
legacy_name rather than name for the prefix: NOPREFIX only ever applies to
legacy (v1) hierarchies, and legacy_name is what cgroup_file_name() itself uses
there, so the symlink matches what userspace actually looks for.

--- a/kernel/cgroup.c
+++ b/kernel/cgroup.c
@@ -3841,10 +3841,23 @@ static int cgroup_add_file(struct cgroup_subsys_state *css, struct cgroup *cgrp,
 	if (cft->file_offset) {
 		struct cgroup_file *cfile = (void *)css + cft->file_offset;
 
 		spin_lock_irq(&cgroup_file_kn_lock);
 		cfile->kn = kn;
 		spin_unlock_irq(&cgroup_file_kn_lock);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * On a NOPREFIX (Android) hierarchy the file above was created as the bare
+	 * cft->name. Add a "<subsys>.<name>" symlink beside it so tools that expect
+	 * the prefixed spelling -- runc in particular -- can find it too.
+	 */
+	if (cft->ss && (cgrp->root->flags & CGRP_ROOT_NOPREFIX) &&
+	    !(cft->flags & CFTYPE_NO_PREFIX)) {
+		snprintf(name, CGROUP_FILE_NAME_MAX, "%s.%s",
+			 cgroup_on_dfl(cgrp) ? cft->ss->name : cft->ss->legacy_name,
+			 cft->name);
+		kernfs_create_link(cgrp->kn, name, kn);
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 }
